{"t":1794304046,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"7977863f-21b3-47e1-8b89-a15cf4cfa43e","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-10T08:57:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 100857\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n357 AM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNothing.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures follow Friday\n     into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain returns Thursday as colder temperatures follow \nFriday into the weekend, with mountain snow and brisk conditions.\n\nShortwave ridging coupled with westerly downslope flow will\nallow for mild afternoons today and Wednesday. High temperatures\ntoday will be around 70. Wednesday will be a few degs cooler \ndue to more clouds and lighter SE winds. Very dry today with \nmarginal fire weather concerns. See Fire Weather section below. \n\nRain is expected to move in by early Thursday as low pressure\ncrosses the Appalachians and over to the Mid-Atlantic coast.\nThere weren't any significant changes seen at the sfc or at  \n500 mb compared to previous runs with the system expected to \nimpact the area Thursday into Friday.\n\nBehind this system, much colder air will follow on brisk NNW\nwinds with the Allegheny Mountains seeing their first \naccumulating snow of the season starting late Thu night and \ncontinuing through Fri evening. A few inches of accumulation are\nlikely falling roughly in a 24-hr period from 06Z Fri through\n06Z Sat. \n\nRemaining colder than normal Fri through the first half of next\nweek under brisk NNW flow between low pressure well offshore and\nstrong Canadian high pressure to our west.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /08Z TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\n\nLighter winds today than yesterday, but potentially still \ngusting 15-20 kt range during the afternoon. Winds shift to the\nsoutheast Wed with even lighter speeds.\n\nRain is expected to arrive by daybreak Thu and continue through\nthe day before it starts exiting to the northeast Thu night, but\nmay linger into Fri morning across northeast Maryland. \n\nBrisk NNW winds gusting 20-30 kt will be the biggest impact\nFri-Sun. MVFR/IFR conditions are likely Thursday into Fri\nmorning with a broken stratocu layer persisting all weekend into\nearly next week.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\n\nWinds remain generally below SCA through Thursday. Winds\nstrengthen Thu night through the weekend and peak in strength\nSaturday with SCA conditions expected Thu night through at least\nthe first part of next week. A period of gale force winds is\npossible Saturday due to tight pressure gradient between deep\nlow offshore and strong Canadian high pressure over the MS River\nValley.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\n\nWarmer today with highs around 70. This when coupled with \ndewpoints in the mid 30s yields MinRh values in the 20s. Winds \nwill be significantly lighter today than yesterday, but may \nstill gust 15 to 20 mph at times. This may be enough to meet the\nSpecial Weather Statement criteria. The area of greatest \nconcern is west of I-95 to the I-81 corridor, which missed much \nof the rain of last Thursday night, nearly a week ago.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/KRR\nAVIATION...LFR/KRR\nMARINE...LFR/KRR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794303993,"days":{"2026-11-10":{"hi":69,"lo":44,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-11":{"hi":68,"lo":49,"pop":20,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-12":{"hi":64,"lo":42,"pop":66,"short":"Light Rain Likely"},"2026-11-13":{"hi":50,"lo":36,"pop":32,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-11-14":{"hi":50,"lo":36,"pop":7,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-15":{"hi":52,"lo":35,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-16":{"hi":54,"lo":null,"pop":1,"short":"Mostly